Description: wait for the phone's first picture, receive whole or fail, and say why a stream ends
 The DroidCam app answers the video request with its 9 byte header at once, but opens the phone's camera only after that, and on a busy
 phone the first frame comes 7 to 18 seconds later (measured with app 6.28 on Android 10, USB through adb and Wi-Fi, the same for the v2 and
 the v3 request). The client gave a running stream and the first frame the same receive timeout set in Connect() (5 seconds, and the
 microseconds select() leaves in the same timeval: about 6 seconds), so it gave up before the first picture, ended its video thread without
 a word and stayed running with nothing written to the video device. The same phone then sent its frames in bursts with gaps of 5 seconds
 and more, which the same timeout would also count as the end of the stream.
 .
 The first frame is now waited for in steps of one second up to 60 seconds, saying once after two seconds that it is waiting. After it
 a running stream is given 15 seconds between frames. When no picture comes, or the stream is lost, the client says so and droidcam-cli
 exits with 3 or 4 (1 for the earlier failures that also left it idle), where it used to wait for ever; the window program does what its
 Stop button does, so it shows Connect again. In -l mode (the phone connects) the client keeps waiting for the next connection as before.
 .
 The video receive is RecvExact(): the header, the frame length and the frame are received whole or the receive fails. A receive that a
 signal interrupts while the program still runs goes on (a stop and continue, Ctrl+Z and fg: SIGCONT interrupts a receive that has a
 timeout, signal(7)), where it ended the stream in silence; only the program's own stop (SIGINT, which clears v_running) is silent. recv()
 with MSG_WAITALL returned fewer bytes than asked after a timeout in the middle of an item, which taken for a whole frame left the stream
 out of step, and a frame length from the stream was never checked against the buffer: it is now (a length of 0 or above the frame buffer
 ends the stream).
 .
 Also: when the v4l2loopback device keeps the size it was first given, -size is not applied (the driver answers the old size to the new
 one); the client says which size the device stays at. When the size the phone sends differs from the size the device takes, the client
 says so once: "the phone sends WxH, shown at WxH".
